        "title": "8. FAQ (12)",
        "content": "8. FAQ (12)\n 1. Are audiology and SLP good careers abroad? Yes — both are respected, in-demand\n clinical fields with rewarding work and strong salaries in many countries.\n 2. What's the difference between audiology and SLP? Audiology focuses on hearing &\n balance; SLP focuses on speech, language, voice, fluency & swallowing. Both are\n skilled clinical professions.\n 3. Do I need a master's or doctorate to practise? Often yes — in many countries a\n master's is the clinical entry-level; in the USA, audiology requires an AuD doctorate.\n\n 4. What licensing do I need? Varies by country — e.g., USA ASHA CCC-A / CCC-SLP +\n state; UK HCPC (+ RCSLT for SLP); Australia SPA / Audiology Australia; Canada SAC\n + provincial. Plus English. We guide you.\n 5. What do audiologists / SLPs earn abroad? It varies widely; our table shows\n illustrative averages for registered clinicians. We share realistic figures in counselling.\n 6. Can I settle abroad with my family? Several countries offer skilled-worker and\n dependent-visa pathways that can lead to settlement — subject to eligibility and\n current rules. No guarantees.\n 7. Which country should I aim for? One where your target discipline is recognised, the\n degree level matches, and registration is achievable — we advise honestly.\n 8. Is English mandatory? Yes — for most overseas registration. IELTS or OET. Academic\n Yatra offers prep.\n 9. Can I work in schools as an SLP? In several countries yes (e.g., USA, UK, Australia) —\n often a strong employer for SLPs.\n10. Do you guarantee a job, visa, or licensure? No — be cautious of anyone who does. We\n provide honest, organised, end-to-end guidance.\n11. What are the eligibility requirements? Typically Class 12 with relevant subjects +\n English and a minimum aggregate; PG programmes require a related undergrad. We\n confirm for you.\n12. How do I start? Book a free counselling session for a personalised audiology/SLP\n roadmap — no obligation.\n Compliance reminder: keep every answer free of job/visa/PR/salary/registration\n guarantees; be honest about degree level and country-by-country recognition."
