        "content": "8. FAQ (12)\n 1. Are EMS careers in demand abroad? Yes — pre-hospital emergency care is essential\n and growing in many countries.\n 2. What's the difference between EMT and Paramedic? EMT is shorter training, basic\n life support; Paramedic is much more advanced, longer study (often a 3-year degree in\n UK/Australia/Canada), broader scope, higher pay.\n 3. Which countries treat Paramedic as a degree-level profession? Notably UK (HCPC),\n Australia (AHPRA) and Canada (provincial) — typically a Bachelor of Paramedicine.\n 4. What certification do I need? Varies — e.g., USA NREMT + state; UK HCPC;\n Australia AHPRA; Canadian provincial. Plus English. We guide you.\n 5. What do EMTs / Paramedics earn abroad? It varies widely; our table shows\n illustrative Paramedic averages — EMT typically earns less, advanced/critical-care\n paramedics often more. We share realistic figures in counselling.\n 6. Can I settle abroad with my family? Several countries (esp. for registered\n Paramedics) offer skilled-worker and dependent-visa pathways — subject to\n eligibility and current rules. No guarantees.\n 7. Is the work physically demanding? Yes — fitness, resilience and shift-work\n suitability matter. We discuss this honestly.\n 8. Can I move from EMT to Paramedic later? In several countries, yes — there are clear\n progression routes. We map them.\n 9. Is English mandatory? For most overseas certification, yes — IELTS or OET.\n Academic Yatra offers prep.\n10. Do you guarantee a job, visa, or certification? No — be cautious of anyone who does.\n We provide honest, organised, end-to-end guidance.\n11. What are the eligibility requirements? Typically Class 12 + English and a minimum\n aggregate; minimum age (often 18+); some countries require fitness checks. We\n confirm for you.\n12. How do I start? Book a free counselling session for a personalised EMS roadmap — no\n obligation.\n\n Compliance reminder: keep every answer free of job/visa/PR/salary/certification\n guarantees; be honest about EMT vs Paramedic differences."
