        "content": "8. FAQ (12)\n 1. Is Prosthetics & Orthotics a good career abroad? Yes — it's a specialised, in-demand\n rehabilitation field with strong salaries in many countries.\n 2. What's the difference between a Prosthetist and an Orthotist? Prosthetists fit\n artificial limbs; orthotists design braces & supports. Many practise as combined\n Prosthetist–Orthotist.\n 3. What registration do I need to practise abroad? Country-specific — e.g., USA\n ABC/BOC (NCOPE-accredited master's + residency); UK HCPC; Australia AOPA;\n Canada CBCPO. Plus English. We guide you.\n 4. What do P&O professionals earn abroad? It varies widely; our table shows illustrative\n averages. We share realistic figures in counselling.\n 5. Can I settle abroad with my family? Several countries offer skilled-worker and\n dependent-visa pathways that can lead to settlement — subject to eligibility and\n current rules. No guarantees.\n 6. Why does accreditation matter? For registration in countries like the USA, your\n programme must usually be accredited by the right body (e.g., NCOPE). Choosing the\n right programme avoids problems later.\n 7. Is the field hands-on? Yes — combining patient assessment, clinical care and device\n fabrication (often with CAD/CAM and 3D printing).\n 8. Is the role technology-heavy? Increasingly yes — 3D printing, advanced materials\n and smart prosthetics are growing the field.\n 9. Is English mandatory? Yes — for most overseas registration. IELTS or OET. Academic\n Yatra offers prep.\n10. Do you guarantee a job, visa, or registration? No — be cautious of anyone who does.\n We provide honest, organised, end-to-end guidance.\n11. What are the eligibility requirements? Typically Class 12 with Science (PCB/PCM as\n allowed) + English and a minimum aggregate; PG programmes need a related\n undergrad. We confirm for you.\n12. How do I start? Book a free counselling session for a personalised P&O roadmap — no\n obligation.\n\n Compliance reminder: keep every answer free of job/visa/PR/salary/registration\n guarantees; be honest about country-by-country accreditation requirements."
